meta {
  name: pairing page (open in a browser)
  type: http
  seq: 1
}

get {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/internal/rcs/pair
  body: none
  auth: none
}

docs {
  **Open this in a browser, not in Bruno** — it is an HTML page, and Bruno will
  only show you the markup.

  This is the whole pairing flow in one page: press a button, scan a QR from the
  phone, watch it confirm. It drives the other requests in this folder for you.

  Open `http://localhost:8080/internal/rcs/pair` in a browser.

  The pairing attempt lives in the server's memory, so every request in the
  flow has to reach the same running process.

  On the phone: Google Messages → profile menu → **Device pairing** → **QR code
  scanner**.

  ## What you need

  An Android phone with RCS / chat features ON, powered and online. It does
  **not** need to be anywhere near the server. The phone talks to Google, we
  talk to Google, and they meet in the middle — exactly like opening
  messages.google.com/web on a laptop in another country.

  ## After it says Paired

  The session is written to the `rcs_sessions` table and the phone connects
  within 30 seconds. It reconnects by itself after a restart or a reschedule,
  with no human involved.

  A QR pairing does not expire the way the cookie path does, so this is a
  one-time job. If the phone offers no QR scanner, use `pair with HAR` instead
  and expect to re-authenticate roughly daily.
}
